      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. The dose looks like it may be a mistake.\n\n**Metformin 1700 mg PO TID = 5,100 mg/day**, which is about double the usual maximum:\n- Typical maximum for immediate-release metformin is **2,550 mg/day** (U.S. labeling; some references allow up to 3,000 mg/day).\n- Typical single doses are **500 to 1,000 mg**, so 1,700 mg per dose is also well above usual.\n- Extended-release maximum is generally 2,000 mg/day.\n\nPatient factors (70 kg, normal renal and hepatic function, no allergies) don't justify a dose above these limits. Excess metformin raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n\nPlease confirm the intended regimen. For example:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the IR maximum)\n- **500 mg PO TID with meals** (1,500 mg/day, a common titration target)\n- **1,000 mg PO BID with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- Or, if you really mean 1,700 mg, tell me the formulation and rationale and I'll enter it with a note.\n\nOnce you confirm, I'll enter the order."
      }
     ],
